{"name":"Ruby Red","hex":"#9b111e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ruby red","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the red colour of a ruby.","Extremely Republican-leaning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ruby_red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruby Red (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Ruby Red is a 2013 German fantasy film based on the book with the same name by Kerstin Gier and is the first part in the Ruby Red Trilogy. A sequel was produced in 2014 under the title Sapphire Blue, followed in 2016 by Emerald Green."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/21/Ruby_Red_%28film%29_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_Red_(film)"},{"title":"Ruby Red (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Ruby Red\" is a song by English rock band Slade, released in 1982 as the third single from the band's tenth studio album, Till Deaf Do Us Part. It was written by lead vocalist Noddy Holder and bassist Jim Lea, and was produced by Slade. \"Ruby Red\" reached number 51 in the UK Singles Chart and remained in the top 75 for three weeks."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e4/Sladesingle-rubyred.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_Red_(song)"},{"title":"Ruby (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Ruby is a color that is a representation of the color of the cut and polished ruby gemstone and is a shade of red or pink."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Ruby_gem.JPG/330px-Ruby_gem.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_(color)"},{"title":"Ruby","blocks":[{"text":"Ruby is a pinkish-red to blood-red-colored gemstone, a variety of the mineral corundum, consisting of aluminium oxide (α-Al2O3). Ruby is one of the most popular traditional jewelry gems and is very durable. Other varieties of gem-quality corundum are called sapphires, and rubies are also sometimes referred to as \"red sapphires\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Corundum-215330.jpg/330px-Corundum-215330.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg/500px-Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg/1280px-Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg/500px-Eisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ballroom in Wartburg Castle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEisenach_Wartburg_Festsaal_04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG/500px-Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG/1280px-Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG/500px-Bayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Markgräfin-Wilhelmine-Gymnasium in Bayreuth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABayreuth-MWGGymnasium-0419a.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/The_Millennium_Bridge%2C_London_-_geograph.o